2026-07-29T03:31:35.3828586Z         "github_event_name": "push",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3829117Z         "github_event_payload": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3829704Z           "after": "5f0802beeb402503cc257d552100de7707ed3651",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3830290Z           "base_ref": null,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3830783Z           "before": "b39be414bd0957dd8574f7aa105e6a17842b92ab",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3831301Z           "commits": [
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3831624Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3831922Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3832318Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3832787Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3833189Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3833496Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3833685Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3833920Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3834182Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3834404Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3834631Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3834822Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3835092Z               "id": "1677551f62e3b7b4d41e1abfc382931cabeb1934",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3845693Z               "message": "Treat DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds as a floor rather than a default\n\nThe floor was standing in for a TTL whenever a response carried none, and\nthat turned out to be the dominant path rather than a corner. Only the\nanswer section was read, so every NXDOMAIN and NODATA looked TTL-less —\nand negative answers are the bulk of what a validation issues: 21\nblocklist zones per MX IP replying \"not listed\", 39 absent DKIM\nselectors, plus the SPF, DMARC, ARC and mail-survey subdomain probes. A\nclean domain is the worst case, because nothing is listed and nothing\nexists. Running DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds=60 against cnn.com therefore\nstamped 60 seconds on most of the cache and dropped hundreds of Redis\nkeys within the first couple of minutes, the floor setting quietly\ndeciding the lifetime of entries no zone had spoken about.\n\nTwo changes, both in DnsCacheTtl:\n\n- A response that published no TTL now yields null, leaving CacheTtlHours\n  governing exactly as it does with gating off. The floor bounds TTLs we\n  received; it is not a substitute for one.\n\n- Negative answers get the TTL the zone actually published for them.\n  RFC 2308 §5 puts it at min(SOA.MINIMUM, TTL of the SOA record) in the\n  authority section, which DnsCacheSerializer was already capturing and\n  persisting — only the TTL policy ignored it. It is clamped by the floor\n  and the cap like any other published TTL, so a short SOA minimum is\n  still raised and a week-long one still held to the ceiling.\n\nWith both, \"no TTL at all\" is genuinely rare: a referral with no SOA, or\na malformed response.\n\nAlso fixes two call sites that read the answer section directly and so\nmissed the SOA: the PTR cache, and QuerySpeculativeAsync, which wrote\nstraight to the cache without passing an entry TTL at all and was giving\nthe DKIM selector and SRV probes the full CacheTtlHours while every gated\npath around them honoured the zone.\n\nNot addressed, and separate: an L2 hit is re-stamped with the cache-wide\nTTL rather than its remaining Redis lifetime, and QuerySpeculativeAsync\nstill does not write through to Redis.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3859379Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:15:28Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3860042Z               "tree_id": "c61778922c0ffefcd5638eb227d5df7ffd5f1aec",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3860650Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/1677551f62e3b7b4d41e1abfc382931cabeb1934"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3861132Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3861319Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3861527Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3861797Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3862103Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3862366Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3862619Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3862830Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3863096Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3863392Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3863646Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3863898Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3864107Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3864385Z               "id": "9bc90747746210facbefb50fa1ebf385f52814e4",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3873821Z               "message": "Bound an L2 hit by the shared key's remaining life\n\nFour paths move a value between L1 and the Redis L2. Three carried the\nlifetime with them; the read leg did not. A hit was cached locally with a\nfresh full CacheTtlHours no matter how little was left on the Redis key,\nso the local copy could outlive the shared entry it came from — and with\nrecord-TTL gating on, badly: a DNS answer bounded to sixty seconds by its\nrecord TTL, read from Redis at fifty-nine seconds old, was then served\nfrom L1 for the full cache TTL.\n\nIt did not stop at L1. SetMemoryOnly records the same expiry in the\nshared-cache key index, so an emptied-Redis re-warm republished the entry\nwith more life than it had ever been given, and the two tiers drifted\nfurther apart on every hit.\n\nTryGetAsync now reads the remaining lifetime alongside the value with\nStringGetWithExpiry — one round trip either way — and returns both, so\nthe expiry cannot be dropped by a future caller. The L1 copy takes the\nsooner of that and a full cache TTL: the shared key bounds us, and a peer\nrunning a longer CacheTtlHours does not license us to extend ours.\n\nUnder CacheTtlHours=0 a hit now takes the shared key's remaining life\nrather than never expiring. Memory not expiring is a statement about\nvalues this instance fetched, not a licence to keep a borrowed one\nforever — and the shared key's lifetime is the only real bound available\nthere.\n\nVerified against a live Redis: the two tests covering the expansion fail\nagainst the previous behaviour and pass now, and the two covering the\ncaps pass either way, guarding the over-correction.\n\nStill outstanding, and not a TTL question: SetMemoryOnly indexes every\nvalue it caches, including results that failed shouldPersist. Those are\ndeliberately kept out of the write-through, but WarmSharedCache reads the\nindex and so can publish a transient error to peers after Redis has been\nemptied.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3881801Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:24:59Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3882171Z               "tree_id": "d223af3a7146f1c34e4cb9c320d781245686e0e4",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3883101Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/9bc90747746210facbefb50fa1ebf385f52814e4"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3884003Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3884189Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3884411Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3884673Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3884991Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3885237Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3885470Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3885659Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3885901Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3886168Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3886641Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3886868Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3887054Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3887344Z               "id": "ea2e08d33c4ed7cef0c42406ebceeeb38b7d405b",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3891666Z               "message": "Record why an L2 hit is capped at our own TTL\n\nThe cap was justified in the comment by a peer running a different\nCacheTtlHours, which is the weakest of the reasons and the rarest. The\nreal one is that Redis outlives a pod: keys written under a longer\nCacheTtlHours survive the deploy that lowered it, so without the cap a\nconfig change would not take effect in L1 until those keys aged out on\nthe old setting. A rolling change has peers on both settings at once for\nthe same reason.\n\nAlso records what the cap does not do, since it invites the opposite\nreading: it bounds how long a value is held, not how stale it may get.\nWhen the L1 copy expires we take an L1 miss, re-read the L2, find the\nsame key still alive and cache it again — total age stays governed by the\nshared key's own lifetime, which is the shared tier's contract to keep.\n\nComments and docs only; no behaviour change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3895473Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:32:44Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3895806Z               "tree_id": "cbd791748ca1bddc718aaa393c259c84fc0e88c8",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3896531Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/ea2e08d33c4ed7cef0c42406ebceeeb38b7d405b"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3896988Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3897173Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3897355Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3897591Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3897868Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3898101Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3898332Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3898524Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3898792Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3899236Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3899474Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3899697Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3899884Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3900274Z               "id": "6a5f3efa4d820f8902d7c4dc0f6f0236701b93d2",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3908022Z               "message": "Keep shouldPersist-rejected values out of the shared-cache key index\n\nThe predicate keeps transient errors out of the disk bag and the L2\nwrite-through. The re-warm is the third way into the shared cache and it\nwas not gated: WarmSharedCache republishes everything the key index\nnames, SetMemoryOnly indexed every value it cached, and the warm cannot\ntell a timeout from a real answer. So a value deliberately withheld from\npeers reached them anyway the first time Redis was emptied — the long way\nround, but the same destination the predicate exists to prevent.\n\nSetMemoryOnly now takes a shareable flag, false only on the transient\npath. That is the one of its three callers whose value is not already\npublished: an L2 hit and a disk import both belong in the index, and a\nre-warm is the only thing that will put them back after a flush.\n\nThe new test fails against the ungated index and passes now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3915726Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:37:02Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3916527Z               "tree_id": "ca73c662c55ebac843626f9bb2006f9a450dfaf8",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3917489Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/6a5f3efa4d820f8902d7c4dc0f6f0236701b93d2"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3918283Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3918584Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3918890Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3919292Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3919758Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3920146Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3920548Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3920867Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3921286Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3921759Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3922157Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3922560Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3922879Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3923360Z               "id": "a619ae3a086f711910ed91002ad74bfec1ebabd0",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3936908Z               "message": "Give the speculative DNS path a real share in the L2\n\nA DKIM selector that does not exist is a perfectly cacheable answer, and\nQuerySpeculativeAsync was persisting it to disk already — but never to\nRedis, on either leg. It wrote with Set, which populated L1, the bag and\nthe key index but not the shared tier; it read with TryGet, which is L1\nonly. So each pod re-probed the same 39 absent selectors, plus the SRV\nand speculative TXT names, and no pod could see another's answer.\n\nFixing only the write would have been pointless: with an L1-only read\nnothing would ever have read what was published.\n\nSet now performs the write-through itself rather than leaving it to the\none caller inside GetOrCreateAsync. Caching a value this process fetched\nmeans the same thing wherever it is called from, and the split is what\nlet this path — and the dead SetQueryCache/SetPtrCache helpers beside it\n— write to two tiers out of three without anything noticing.\n\nTryGetSharedAsync is the read half for callers that cannot use\nGetOrCreateAsync. The speculative path cannot: it must not cache its own\ntimeouts, and GetOrCreateAsync caches every factory result in L1 one way\nor another, which would leave a 3-second miss shadowing the longer\nQueryAsync behind it. A recheck misses the L2 as well as L1, exactly as\nGetOrCreateAsync arranges.\n\nThe two write-through tests fail against the old split and pass now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3946736Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:44:22Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3947087Z               "tree_id": "6ff6c85fc86e44d974d33409b31795909fe1dca8",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3947599Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/a619ae3a086f711910ed91002ad74bfec1ebabd0"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3948041Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3948221Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3948597Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3948840Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3949119Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3949353Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3949585Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3949777Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3950019Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3950288Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3950522Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3950749Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3950938Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3951214Z               "id": "903c36e83f8a970bee71cd2d9565683cb8e3c437",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3958137Z               "message": "Run the Redis-backed tests in CI, and prune five dead cache helpers\n\nCI had no Redis, so every Redis-backed test self-skipped there. The last\nrun on main reported 315 of 315 passed while logging \"SKIPPED: no Redis\"\n43 times — the shared L2, the emptied-cache re-warm, L2 lifetimes, the\nconfig and auth beacons and the write leases all unverified by the thing\nthat gates merges. Every shared-cache fix on this branch would have gone\ngreen whether or not it worked.\n\nThe build job now runs a redis:7-alpine service on 6380, the port the\ntests use (6379 is left alone so a developer's own Redis stays out of the\ntest keyspace).\n\nRedisTestPrerequisiteTests keeps it fixed. The self-skip is right for a\ndev box and must stay, so the guard is opt-in: with EDNSV_REQUIRE_REDIS=1,\nset only on the CI test step, an unreachable Redis fails with a message\nnaming the consequence. Removing or breaking the service container can no\nlonger quietly return dozens of tests to skipping. Verified in all three\nstates — unset skips, set with Redis passes, set without fails.\n\nSeparately, five private helpers in DnsResolverService had no callers:\nSetQueryCache, SetPtrCache, SetServerQueryCache, TryGetPtrCache and\nTryGetServerQueryCache. The three Set ones called ProbeCache.Set with no\nentry TTL, which is the un-gated write that this branch has just finished\nremoving from the speculative path — a ready-made way to reintroduce it.\nTryGetQueryCache has three callers and stays.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3964036Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:48:03Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3964386Z               "tree_id": "4ae6aa8ead5e2ac312763fca54351b6fb60f138a",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3964894Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/903c36e83f8a970bee71cd2d9565683cb8e3c437"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3965340Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3965518Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3965701Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3965940Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3966491Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3966859Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3967109Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3967303Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3967548Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3967822Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3968051Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3968279Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3968464Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3968963Z               "id": "7e859ee843dd584e0e947f76d9051a2f8a142126",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3982043Z               "message": "Tell a failed lookup apart from an answer, and stop caching it for hours\n\nRepeated runs against cnn.com produced spurious findings after about a\nminute: IPs with perfectly good reverse DNS reported as \"No PTR record —\nmany receivers reject mail from IPs without reverse DNS\". Three defects\ncompounded, one of them introduced on this branch.\n\nAn error response was treated as an answer. The persistence predicates\nrejected only EmptyResponse.Instance, the sentinel substituted when a\nquery threw — but every client runs with ThrowDnsErrors=false, so a\nSERVFAIL never throws. It arrived as an ordinary response object and was\ncached and persisted as though the zone had answered. The documentation\nalready claimed these predicates skipped \"DNS errors\"; only transport\nerrors were ever skipped, so IsAnswer closes a gap between the code and\nits stated contract rather than changing the intent. Definitive means\nNoError or NXDomain: both are things the zone told us, both carry an SOA,\nand caching them is the point.\n\nThat gap was made much worse here. A SERVFAIL carries no answers and no\nSOA, so it publishes no TTL — and once \"no published TTL\" came to mean\n\"inherit CacheTtlHours\" (1677551), one failed lookup went from 60 seconds\nto two hours, on disk, and via the write-through folded into Set\n(a619ae3) shared with every peer.\n\nA rejected value took the cache-wide TTL. The intent was recorded as\n\"avoid repeated network calls for the rest of the current process\", which\nis one run for the CLI and days for the web service. So a transient\nfailure was held for the whole CacheTtlHours while a successful answer\nunder record-TTL gating might live sixty seconds — the failure outliving\nthe success by two orders of magnitude, and every refetch another chance\nto acquire one. Rejected values now expire on\nProbeCachePolicy.TransientLifetime (30s), never later than a normal entry\nwould. The in-flight map already collapses concurrent duplicates, so the\nwindow only ever needed to span one validation. Both cache variants, so a\ntimed-out port probe stops reporting a shut port.\n\nA failed PTR lookup was an empty list, indistinguishable from an IP that\ngenuinely has no PTR — which is how a timeout reached users as a\nmail-delivery warning. ResolvePtrAsync now returns a distinguished\ninstance recognised by reference, and the six consumers report \"reverse\nlookup failed — not checked\" instead of counting it as a finding. A\nsentinel rather than a wrapper type because the value is persisted as a\nList<string>; a failure is never persisted, so it only has to survive a\ncache hit, which a reference does.\n\nThree of the seven new tests fail against the previous behaviour, one per\ndefect; the rest pin the invariants that must not move — an accepted\nvalue still takes the cache TTL, and the transient window is a ceiling on\nfailures rather than a floor.\n\nNot changed: the unreachable-server breaker still covers QueryServerAsync\nalone. The docs now say so, and say why the recursive path is left to\nreport errors rather than silently skip.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3993802Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T03:31:23Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3994135Z               "tree_id": "1c0eb1fa186e741504099d523360020481295a09",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3994630Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/7e859ee843dd584e0e947f76d9051a2f8a142126"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3995071Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3995246Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3995426Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3995664Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3995941Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3996171Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3996620Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3996811Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3997200Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3997475Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3997702Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3997925Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3998117Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.3998427Z               "id": "ab867c4c7b4cdcdf031940c2328306b971f384a7",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4004151Z               "message": "Show which build is answering\n\nA deployment question was mistaken for a code question: a fix sat on an\nunmerged branch, pods were restarted, the symptom persisted — and nothing\na running instance exposed could tell \"not deployed\" apart from \"does not\nwork\". Both look exactly like the original bug.\n\nThe commit now appears in three places, cheapest first: the startup log,\nso `kubectl logs` answers it without reaching the app; /api/defaults,\nwhich the UI already fetches; and a quiet stamp beside the subtitle in\nthe validator UI, with the full hash as its tooltip and click-to-copy.\n\nBuildInfo reads AssemblyInformationalVersion, which the SDK writes as\n1.0.0+<commit>. That works locally because the SDK reads the checkout —\nbut the Dockerfile copies sources without .git, so every image would have\nreported a bare version and no revision, which is precisely the case that\nneeded identifying. It now takes a SOURCE_COMMIT build argument through\nto /p:SourceRevisionId, and CI passes github.sha. Verified by publishing\nwith the override and reading back the stamped attribute.\n\nAn absent revision is reported as \"no commit\" rather than hidden or\nfaked: a build that did not carry its provenance is itself worth seeing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4009550Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-28T05:23:48Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4009886Z               "tree_id": "e0aeac1ec62ea05d3e3bc779319f90fd569f83ad",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4010399Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/ab867c4c7b4cdcdf031940c2328306b971f384a7"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4010828Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4011005Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4011187Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4011422Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4011696Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4011939Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4012166Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4012351Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4012599Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4012865Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4013091Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4013312Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4013497Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4013816Z               "id": "84d117513c9bc36e2e019625305dbdc516ddd2f1",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4021916Z               "message": "Cap negative answers well below positive ones, and stamp resolvable provenance\n\nDnsNegativeTtlCapSeconds (default 600) is a second ceiling that applies to\nNXDOMAIN and NODATA alone. The asymmetry is the point: a stale \"this\nexists\" merely goes out of date, while a stale \"this does not exist\"\nbecomes a false finding — \"No PTR record, Gmail will reject mail\" against\nan IP with perfectly good reverse DNS. cnn.com's reverse zones publish an\nSOA MINIMUM of 86400, so honouring it meant one possibly-wrong NXDOMAIN\nwas recorded for the full CacheTtlHours, persisted to disk and shared with\nevery pod. Ten minutes bounds that to something that self-corrects.\n\nIt is a ceiling rather than part of the record-TTL gating, so unlike the\nfloor it applies whether or not DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds is set — gating\nships off, and the damage this bounds does not depend on it. The cache TTL\nremains the outer ceiling either way, since the sweep still deletes a\nrecord file at fileTime + CacheTtlHours. Positive answers are untouched.\n\nRechecks already bypass this, as they bypass every cached read: the cap\nshortens an entry's life, a recheck ignores its life entirely. Pinned by a\ntest rather than left to inference.\n\nAlso fixes the build stamp to name a commit that exists. On pull_request\nevents github.sha is the ephemeral merge commit GitHub synthesises for the\nrun — it belongs to no branch and cannot be looked up afterwards, which is\nexactly what happened: a reported version resolved to nothing and left the\ndeployed code unidentifiable, the one question the stamp exists to answer.\nIt now uses the PR head SHA, and carries branch and PR number alongside\nvia assembly metadata, shown in the startup log, /api/defaults and the UI\ntooltip.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4028975Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-29T00:23:39Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4029307Z               "tree_id": "3a19e53da7abeea1319b371d9d7f15a40958388a",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4029805Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/84d117513c9bc36e2e019625305dbdc516ddd2f1"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4030235Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4030407Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4030588Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4030821Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4031092Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4031326Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4031552Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4031741Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4031978Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4032243Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4032469Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4032692Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4032881Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4033163Z               "id": "bff4ff0fdade3fbc889bee5a440bb78a4195f264",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4041445Z               "message": "Document the cache behaviour map, and what a negative answer actually is\n\nDnsNegativeTtlCapSeconds is easy to read as \"how long errors are cached\",\nwhich is the opposite of what it does. Every description of it now leads\nwith the correction: it is a TTL for a positive non-result. The lookup\nsucceeded and established that something does not exist — an absent DKIM\nselector, an IP with no PTR, a \"not listed\" blocklist reply, a name with\nno CAA. That is a result the zone published, with an SOA saying how long\nto believe it, and it is cached and persisted exactly like a record.\nTimeouts and SERVFAIL are failures, a different bucket with a fixed 30s\nthat never reaches disk or Redis.\n\nNew docs/cache-behaviour-map.md maps the three result classes onto what\nthe checks actually ask for: a decision diagram from query to RCODE to\nlifetime, a comparison table, the 87 checks grouped by cache and record\ntype with what a non-result means for each family, and a tier diagram\nshowing that failures stop at L1.\n\nIts edge-case table covers the cases most likely to surprise: the cap\napplies with gating off (it is a ceiling, not part of the gating, and\ngating ships off so that is the default path); it is never a floor, so a\nshorter SOA minimum wins; the DnsCacheMinTtlSeconds floor still raises a\nvery short negative TTL; a negative answer with no SOA takes the cap\nrather than inheriting CacheTtlHours; CacheTtlHours stays the outer\nceiling; CacheTtlHours=0 does not exempt negatives; rechecks bypass it\nentirely; and what it cannot do — it bounds how long a wrong negative\nsurvives, it cannot make a resolver answer correctly.\n\nAlso corrects service-layer.md, which still described shouldPersist as\nskipping \"DNS errors\" and holding failures \"for the rest of the current\nprocess\" — both true of the intent and neither true of the code until\nthis branch.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4048864Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-29T01:39:43Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4049207Z               "tree_id": "04bab31e01fadb53c4c35888fb6c82bbe92ddb2c",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4049719Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/bff4ff0fdade3fbc889bee5a440bb78a4195f264"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4050161Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4050333Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4050509Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4050741Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4051152Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4051385Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4051610Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4051797Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4052036Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4052302Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4052531Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4052757Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4052950Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4053224Z               "id": "c881c3aeec88cee767448511dc42aef4172ae6dd",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4060221Z               "message": "Cover every check individually in the cache behaviour map\n\nThe map grouped checks by category with counts, which answered \"roughly\nhow does DKIM cache\" but not \"this check gave me an odd answer — where\ndid it go\". All 87 are now listed one per row with the record types they\nquery, the caches those land in, and the CacheDep flags a recheck of that\ncategory bypasses.\n\nRecord types include the ones reached indirectly through CheckContext: a\ncheck reading ctx.MxHosts depends on the cached MX lookup even though it\nissues no query of its own, and omitting that would make a third of the\ntable look probe-free.\n\nThree sub-sections carry the parts a row cannot: what each cache is and\nwhich tiers it reaches (_portCache, _rcptCache, _relayCache and _axfrCache\nare L1 and disk only, never shared between pods); the three families where\na negative answer is the routine outcome rather than a problem, which is\nwhere the negative cap earns its keep; and the PTR exception, the one\ncache whose callers can tell a failure from an absence, with what each of\nthe six consumers prints in either case.\n\nCacheBehaviourMapTests keeps it honest. A table claiming to cover every\ncheck is worse than none once it falls behind, because it reads as\nauthoritative and the checks it omits are the ones nobody thought about.\nIt matches names against table cells rather than by substring — the first\nversion passed happily when a row was renamed \"Open Relay TestX\", since\nthat still contains \"Open Relay Test\".\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4066110Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-29T01:49:50Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4066754Z               "tree_id": "f1b879713de467f55b12fb95ab7e04508189b303",
