2026-07-29T03:31:35.4085751Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4086027Z               "id": "1988fb6fd548b4b8eb9cbbc334c669830d126585",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4092591Z               "message": "Write the cache behaviour map as reference, not as a changelog\n\nThe first draft was organised around one recent change: a section devoted\nto DnsNegativeTtlCapSeconds, a worked example naming cnn.com and a\nspecific IP, before-and-after narration, and prose explaining which\nsetting is \"most likely to be misread\". That is postmortem material. A\nreference page should describe how the system behaves, and it dates badly\nthe moment the change stops being recent.\n\nRestructured around the durable shape: three kinds of result, then the\nlifetimes that apply to each, then the per-check table, then the tiers.\nThe negative cap is now one row in a settings table and one paragraph of\nrationale, sitting alongside CacheTtlHours, the floor, the transient\nwindow and the unreachable-server decay rather than above them.\n\nThe edge-case table stays — the interactions between floor, cap and\nCacheTtlHours are genuinely non-obvious — but as \"interactions worth\nknowing\" for the lifetime rules as a whole rather than as a defence of one\nsetting. Concrete incident details are gone; the general form of the\nadvice (compare the configured resolver against a public one) is kept\nbecause it is useful independently of what prompted it.\n\nNet 136 lines removed, 85 added. All 87 checks and the completeness guard\nare unaffected.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4098261Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-29T01:52:28Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4098606Z               "tree_id": "c514ed9dc7b07a92f502962866108eb5f53f2b0b",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4099108Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/1988fb6fd548b4b8eb9cbbc334c669830d126585"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4099539Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4099717Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4099897Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4100140Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4100424Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4100660Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4100888Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4101075Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4101315Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4101582Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4101813Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4102039Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4102224Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4102503Z               "id": "a466b46b37ffba939f73b4183a16c3af3609ca89",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4109481Z               "message": "Map the whole cache path visually, and scope the DNS classification as DNS\n\nThe three-way positive / negative / failure split is a property of DNS,\nnot of caching in general, but it was presented as the doc's opening\npremise — which left SMTP, HTTP and zone-transfer results undescribed and\nimplied a taxonomy they do not share.\n\nA new opening diagram carries the whole path in one view: all 87 checks\ngrouped by what they probe, the record type or protocol each uses, how\nthat protocol's replies are classified, which of the nine caches holds\nthe result, and the lifetime and reach that follow. The DNS section keeps\nits own diagram and stays detailed — it is the primary concern and most of\nthe traffic — but now as the first of three result models rather than the\nonly one.\n\nThe other two are stated rather than left implicit. SMTP, HTTP and AXFR\nhave no negative class: nothing in those protocols publishes \"this does\nnot exist, for the next hour\". They split definitive / transient, and the\ntwo cases worth knowing are that an HTTP 404 is a definitive answer (there\nis genuinely no MTA-STS policy there) while a status 0 is not, and that a\nfailed zone transfer is never recorded at all, because reduced to a\nboolean it is indistinguishable from a refused one.\n\nGroup counts in the diagram are computed from the check table rather than\ncounted by hand — the first draft had 46 record-lookup checks against an\nactual 48, and filed Certificate Transparency under web endpoints when its\ncategory is CAA.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4115251Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-29T02:03:17Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4115576Z               "tree_id": "4745afb4283017398cfdb5dc2fc5d6d9c29b6781",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4116066Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/a466b46b37ffba939f73b4183a16c3af3609ca89"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4116896Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4117079Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4117261Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4117498Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4117771Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4118004Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4118229Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4118421Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4118664Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4118933Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4119158Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4119379Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4119565Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4119843Z               "id": "0d66cdcccbaba444fae3514c59c8f05c00d7bde6",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4127104Z               "message": "Draw the cache path per check, top to bottom\n\nThe overview diagram grouped 87 checks into eight boxes and drew generic\narrows between layers, which described the shape of the system without\nanswering anything about a specific check. Replaced with eight top-to-\nbottom diagrams — one per probe family — carrying every check as its own\nnode.\n\nEach follows the DNS flow's shape: check → cache → result class → the\nlifetime formula and which tiers it reaches. Edge labels carry the record\ntypes that check reaches through that cache, including the ones it gets\nindirectly through CheckContext rather than querying itself, so a check\nthat issues no query of its own still shows what it depends on.\n\nThe result terminals are per-family rather than shared, because the\nclasses differ: the DNS caches fan out to positive / negative / failure\nwith the clamp expressions, while _probeCache and _getCache split\ndefinitive / transient, and _portCache, _rcptCache, _relayCache and\n_axfrCache split the same way but terminate in \"L1 + disk, never Redis\".\n\nGenerated from the check table rather than written by hand, and validated:\n227 edges across eight diagrams, every node reference and style target\nresolving within its own diagram. Two generator faults were caught that\nway — a dropped closing fence, and styles leaking across diagrams because\nthe node-definition scan ran over the cumulative output instead of the\ncurrent one.\n\nSection 2 keeps the DNS decision flow, retitled to sit under the expanded\nview rather than duplicate it; the full table stays as section 5 for\nsearching and diffing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4133218Z               "timestamp": "2026-07-29T02:54:47Z",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4133538Z               "tree_id": "95dc12280a727845d3d35e165557cd18e58c4330",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4134034Z               "url": "https://github.com/pkinerd/ednsv/commit/0d66cdcccbaba444fae3514c59c8f05c00d7bde6"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4134468Z             },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4134642Z             {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4134820Z               "author": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4135052Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4135324Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4135550Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4135776Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4135963Z               "committer": {
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4136386Z                 "email": "noreply@anthropic.com",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4136805Z                 "name": "Claude",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4137037Z                 "username": "claude"
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4137258Z               },
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4137446Z               "distinct": false,
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4137717Z               "id": "c442cfb0b13473b36aa73a79b4feb9cb8dbe8d1e",
2026-07-29T03:31:35.4144311Z               "message": "Reconcile the cache map against the code, and correct three caches\n\nReconciling the diagrams and tables against the service code found a\nclaim that was wrong in all of them: that every cache holds a transient\nfailure for 30 seconds in L1.\n\nThat is true only of ProbeCache and ProbeCacheValue, which have the\nshouldPersist predicate and the transient window. _rcptCache, _relayCache\nand _axfrCache are an ExpiringMap, which has neither — their callers just\ndo not write on a transient failure, so nothing is cached at all and the\nnext request refetches. The doc gave those three a lifetime they have no\nmechanism to implement, which reads entirely plausible and is the reason\nit survived several passes.\n\nThe eight diagrams now carry a fourth result terminal for that case, and\nboth prose tables are corrected. The cache table gains a Type column,\nbecause the wrapper type is what actually decides the Redis and transient\ncolumns — stating it makes the other two checkable rather than asserted.\n\nTwo guards added. A reconciliation pass compares three independent\nsources — the diagrams, the per-check table, and the cache fields in\nDnsResolverService, SmtpProbeService and HttpProbeService — on cache\nmembership per check, Redis reach, and transient behaviour; it reports\n87 checks in the table, 87 in the diagrams, and agreement across all ten\ncaches. TheCacheTableMatchesTheWrapperType pins the same invariant as a\ntest, and was confirmed to fail against the original wording.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_014SVV2X6y5grwQLEV6xuUQz",
